direction: right

title: The state of a table buffer {
  near: top-center
  shape: text
  style.font-size: 24
}

classes: {
  state: {style.fill: "#2d2d4a"}
  data: {style.fill: "#3d3d3d"}
}

state: |md
  ### state.lua
  `csv.State`, one per buffer. The file's
  columns, and everything the user has
  asked for on top of them.
| {class: state}

fields: ||md
  | Field | Holds |
  |---|---|
  | `columns` | Every source column, in display order, hidden ones among them |
  | `rowid_column` | The row id the pipeline prepends, `column_id` -1 |
  | `filters` | ANDed together |
  | `sort_keys` | Most significant first |
  | `marked` | Row ids |
  | `marked_columns` | Column ids |
  | `formats` | Keyed by column id |
  | `selection` | The anchor cell, the head cell and the kind |
  | `page`, `limit` | Which slice of rows |
|| {class: data}

columns: |md
  ### columns.lua
  What a column is: `name` as the file
  spells it, a unique `label`, `column_id`,
  `hidden`. A repeated name takes a `[n]`
  suffix in its label, so `rename` can
  address it.

  Also which columns the table draws.
  `display_columns` takes the visible ones
  from `state.columns`. Hide, cut, paste and
  swap all work on that one list.
| {class: state}

format: |md
  ### format.lua
  From a 600 row sample: int, float with a
  decimal count, date, or text. The user
  can override the width, the alignment
  and the printf spec.
| {class: state}

selection: |md
  ### selection.lua
  `csv.Selection`: an `anchor` cell, a `head`
  cell, and a `kind` saying which of the two
  axes those cells bound. `row` takes every
  column, `column` takes every row.

  `buffer.render` clears it, so both ends
  belong to the layout on screen.

  `movement.lua` is what moves the head: a
  shift keyed move, or any move made while
  nvim is in a visual mode.
| {class: state}

state -> fields
state -> columns: identity, and which are drawn
state -> format: how each one reads
state -> selection: which cells are picked
